{
  "image_name": "7d0af99ec0e75daddc050c24f2560e46.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several data-driven strategies used in materials research. One part compares two ways of choosing experiments in a two-parameter design space. Uniform grid sampling places points on a regular lattice, while Latin hypercube sampling spreads points more irregularly so that each region of each parameter axis is sampled without strict alignment; a labeled distance highlights how spacing between sampled points matters.\n\nAnother part shows an active-learning loop for machine learning–guided experimentation. Starting from known datasets of materials and property values, a model is trained, used to predict properties for possible new experiments, and then an acquisition step selects the next experiment. The new result is fed back into the dataset, creating an iterative cycle that appears aimed at efficiently improving predictions and guiding materials design.\n\nThe bottom section illustrates a text-mining pipeline for building a materials database from published articles. Journal articles in XML or HTML are first cleaned into plain text, then processed with NLP methods such as tokenization, part-of-speech tagging, named entity recognition, phrase parsing, and information extraction. The final output is a structured table linking each material to processing conditions and measured properties.",
